The Anatomy of Passion: Love, Jealousy, and RevengeAThroughout human history, poets, playwrights, and philosophers have treated romantic love, jealousy, and revenge as deeply intertwined forces of the human condition. From the ancient Greek tragedies of Medea to William Shakespeare’s Othello, narrative traditions across cultures have repeatedly illustrated how intense affection can curdle into suspicion, and how perceived betrayal can provoke calculated retaliation. For centuries, these emotional transitions were viewed primarily as moral failings or artistic tropes. In recent decades, however, evolutionary psychologists and neurobiologists have begun examining these three states as interconnected biological mechanisms shaped by human survival and social bonding.BFrom an evolutionary perspective, romantic love—often termed "pair-bonding"—evolved to keep two parents together long enough to raise vulnerable human offspring. Unlike many mammals whose young become independent within weeks or months, human infants require years of intensive care and resource provision. Neuroimaging studies conducted by biological anthropologist Helen Fisher and her colleagues have demonstrated that early-stage romantic love activates the brain’s ventral tegmental area (VTA), flooding the neural pathways with dopamine. This chemical surge creates intense focus, energy, and motivation directed toward a single partner, effectively discouraging individuals from seeking alternative mates during critical periods of child-rearing.CYet the very mechanism that fosters deep attachment also creates profound vulnerability. Evolutionary theorists argue that romantic jealousy emerged as an adaptive "mate-retention" strategy—an emotional alarm system triggered by the perceived threat of infidelity or abandonment. Studies led by psychologist David Buss suggest that jealousy is not a single uniform emotion, but rather one that manifests differently depending on the nature of the threat. While both men and women experience acute distress over a partner's betrayal, research indicates subtle statistical differences in what triggers the strongest reaction: emotional detachment versus sexual infidelity. Regardless of the trigger, jealousy stimulates the amygdala—the brain's threat-detection center—shifting an individual from a state of calm attachment into hyper-vigilance.DWhen jealousy confirms—or appears to confirm—that a social or romantic contract has been broken, the resulting psychological pain often gives way to a desire for revenge. Neuroscientist Dominique de Quervain discovered that when individuals contemplate punishing someone who has betrayed their trust, the dorsal striatum—a region associated with reward and pleasure—becomes highly active. In effect, the brain anticipates that retaliation will restore emotional equilibrium and deter future exploitation. In ancestral environments where formal legal systems did not exist, a reputation for avenging betrayal served as a crucial deterrent against social and romantic poaching.EDespite their evolutionary roots, these emotional reflexes frequently misfire in the modern world. Because the human brain reacts to perceived threats almost identically to actual ones, misinterpretations, digital surveillance on social media, and unfounded rumors can activate the jealousy-revenge cycle without any real betrayal having occurred. Furthermore, psychological research consistently shows that while the anticipation of revenge activates reward centers, the actual execution of retaliation rarely brings lasting relief. Instead, studies conducted by social psychologist Kevin Carlsmith reveal that individuals who carry out revenge often ruminate more on the offense afterward, prolonging their distress rather than resolving it.FUnderstanding the biological sequence linking love, jealousy, and revenge has significant implications for modern conflict resolution and relationship therapy. Rather than viewing jealousy or vengeful impulses as signs of irredeemable character flaws, clinicians increasingly frame them as outdated survival instincts operating in a contemporary environment. By recognizing the physiological lag between an emotional trigger and rational assessment, individuals can interrupt the escalation from attachment anxiety to destructive retaliation.Questions 1–7Do the following statements agree with the information given in the Reading Passage?Write:TRUE if the statement agrees with the informationFALSE if the statement contradicts the informationNOT GIVEN if there is no information on thisHistorically, love, jealousy, and revenge were studied mainly from a biological perspective.Human children need parental support for a longer period than the offspring of most other mammals.Dopamine released during early romantic love encourages people to look for multiple partners.Men and women always experience the exact same level of jealousy regardless of the type of betrayal.Brain scans show that planning revenge stimulates areas linked to enjoyment.In early human societies, retaliating against betrayal helped discourage others from interfering in relationships.Carrying out an act of revenge generally leaves people feeling permanently satisfied.Questions 8–13Complete the summary below.Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.How Emotional Reflexes Operate TodayAlthough love, jealousy, and revenge developed as survival mechanisms, they often malfunction in contemporary society. The human brain struggles to distinguish between real danger and 8 ____________________, meaning modern habits like checking 9 ____________________ or hearing false rumors can trigger the cycle unnecessarily.Research by Kevin Carlsmith indicates that taking revenge actually causes people to 10 ____________________ on the original hurt for longer, rather than helping them move on. Today, therapists view these reactions not as permanent 11 ____________________, but as primitive 12 ___________________